  "title": "Vector Functions: Velocity and Acceleration",
  "eyebrow": "vector-valued functions",
  "language": "en",
  "duration": 41,
  "topic": "A vector function r(t) = (x(t), y(t)) traces a curve. Its derivative r'(t) is the velocity — always tangent to the path, with length equal to the speed. The second derivative r''(t) is the acceleration. On the ellipse r(t) = (2.4 cos t, 1.4 sin t) the acceleration equals -r(t), so it always points back toward the centre, bending the motion into the loop.",
